            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="MAX01" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>William</forename>
                        <surname>Maxwell</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1768">
                        <placeName> </placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1833"/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Maxwell was a friend and
    supporter of <persName key="TOO01">Horne Tooke</persName> at the Westminster election of 1796. He joined the Whig club in 1797. He
    seems to have been a member of <persName key="GER01">Gerrald's</persName> Committee in 1795, along with <persName key="MAC01">Mackintosh</persName>, Godwin,
    <persName key="FER01">Fergusson</persName>, <persName key="PER01">Perry</persName>, and <persName key="HOL01">Holcroft</persName>. 
    In 1796 he is encountered at <persName key="LAU01">Lauderdale's</persName> and <persName key="DEB01">Debrett's</persName>, and in
    1797 at Tooke's and <persName key="BUR02">Burdett's</persName> - the two venues for the meetings in 1806. The 1827 meeting is a 'Call
    on'.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="printedbook"> R.G. Thorne, <title>The Commons 1790-1820</title>, 5 vols (London: Secker and Warburg for 
    the History of Parliament Trust, 1986).</bibl>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
